@startuml shared_filter_signal_flow
!theme cyborg
!pragma layout smetana
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam component {
 BackgroundColor<<candidate>> #2b2f1d
 BorderColor<<candidate>> #b8a14a
 FontColor<<candidate>> #e8dca8
 BackgroundColor<<optional>> #273241
 BorderColor<<optional>> #61738a
 FontColor<<optional>> #b8c4d4
}
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam defaultFontName SansSerif
title Which filter does each path apply?
left to right direction
component "INMP441 RX\nnormalized mono blocks\n16 kHz original rate" as source
component "Branch point\nfan-out" as branch <<candidate>>
package "Analysis path · BPM worker" {
 component "Analysis pool copy\nbefore volume\n(4 blocks: candidate)" as pool <<candidate>>
 component "BPM filter\nalways applied,\nown instance" as analysisFilter
 component "Envelope · periodicity\n· windows · estimates\n(draft algorithm)" as estimator
}
package "Listening path · audio thread" {
 component "Switch position\nRaw: no filter\nMurmur: Murmur filter\nBPM: BPM filter, hears what\nthe analysis gets (same\ncoefficients, own state)" as listeningFilter
 component "Volume\n(potentiometer)" as volume
 component "Saturation\nat output conversion" as saturation
 component "PCM5102A TX\nline output" as sink
}
component "Capture tap\nraw, before filter / volume" as capture <<optional>>
source --> branch
branch --> pool
pool --> analysisFilter
analysisFilter --> estimator
branch --> listeningFilter
listeningFilter --> volume
volume --> saturation
saturation --> sink
branch --> capture
note bottom of analysisFilter
 Independent of the switch: BPM
 is estimated while the listener
 uses Raw, Murmur or BPM.
end note
legend bottom
 Filter study TICKET-189 chooses the BPM and Murmur coefficients;
 it does not decide whether analysis follows the switch (it never does).
 Volume and saturation sit after the branch, so they never change the analysis input.
 Yellow = candidate (not decided). Grey = optional beyond the MVP. BPM / filter code is a skeleton.
endlegend
@enduml
